.TH CTPOOL_FUTURE_GET 3 "2026-09-21" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ctpool_future_get \- block until the future has a result
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

void *ctpool_future_get(ctpool_future *f);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Blocks until the task of this future stops, and then returns the void*
value that the task function returned. If ctpool_shutdown_immediate
cancelled the task, the return value is NULL and
ctpool_future_cancelled(f) returns true.

The call doesn't free the future; the caller must call
ctpool_future_free after reading the result.

Any number of threads can be inside this call on one future at the
same time, and all of them get the same result. Each one holds its own
reference for the length of its call, so the future outlives every
waiter inside it, whatever order the other references go in. The
task's reference goes when the task stops, the caller's goes with
ctpool_future_free, and whichever thread leaves the future last
destroys it.

Result ownership: the pool never allocates, copies or frees the void*
that comes back, it only carries the pointer that the task function
returned. ctpool_future_free releases the future's handle, never the
result. Once this function returns the caller owns the result, and
must free it the same way the task function allocated it.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
f   Future handle (NULL is a no-op that returns NULL)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
The void* value returned by the task function, or NULL if the future
was cancelled or f is NULL.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_submit_future (3),
.BR ctpool_future_done (3),
.BR ctpool_future_cancelled (3),
.BR ctpool_future_free (3)
